mission "An Old Captain's Farewell"
    category "intro"
    name "An Old Captain's Farewell"
    description "Carry Captain Elias Ward's final sea chest and letter to his old quartermaster in Tortuga."
    source "Port Royal"
    destination "Tortuga"
    reward 120
    speaker offer "Captain Elias Ward"
    speaker visit "Mara Bell"
    speaker complete "Mara Bell"
    on offer
        conversation
            `An old captain sits near the harbour office window, folding a faded chart with slow, careful hands. His coat is clean but worn at the cuffs, and a small brass compass rests beside his cup.`
            `"Elias Ward," he says when you approach. "Forty years under sail, and tomorrow I mean to wake up with no deck beneath my feet. Before I go, I promised an old quartermaster in Tortuga that she would have my sea chest and the letter inside it. My knees are done with the sea. Yours, by the look of you, are not."`
            `He studies your little ship through the window. "You seem new to command. No shame in that. A ship is a stubborn creature, and the sea is a patient teacher. Ask what you like; I have time enough for one last lesson."`
            label Question
            choice
                `"How do I handle the ship?"`
                    goto sailing
                `"Where does a captain find work and supplies?"`
                    goto work
                `"What should I know about treasure and the chart?"`
                    goto treasure
                `"And if another ship comes looking for trouble?"`
                    goto fighting
                `"I think I understand. Tell me about this sea chest."`
                    goto the_job
            label sailing
                `"W raises your canvas; S takes it in. A and D turn your bow. Do not expect a ship to turn on a coin: she carries her way through the water, and the wind matters. When the wind is against you, O puts the oars out. They will pull you along even when the canvas sulks. E is for making fast at a pier or casting off again. Come in slowly; no harbour likes a captain who arrives at full speed."`
                goto Question
            label work
                `"Q brings up the harbour office. Ask after work and you will hear of proper ventures; the notice board is for ordinary business. There may be several postings: choose one, or take a handful if their courses suit. A shipwright can sell you a better hull or mend the one you have. Press B while ashore. M opens the sea atlas, and J keeps your journal. A captain who reads his chart saves more than a captain who only reads the wind."`
                goto Question
            label treasure
                `"Keep an eye on the water for bottles and odd scraps of chart. T lets you study the maps you have found; R is how you recover a bottle or search a marked place when close enough. Some clues are riddles. A wrong guess will not ruin the chart, so use your head and try again."`
                goto Question
            label fighting
                `"Z fires the guns to port, X to starboard. Give the broadside time to work; every cannon has its own temper once the first volley is away. If you keep company, F tells your escorts to fight, H holds their fire, G gathers them close, and V puts them back in sailing order. A lone captain can survive a bad day. A captain who watches his fleet may win one."`
                goto Question
            label the_job
                `"Mara Bell keeps a chandlery in Tortuga. She sailed with me for twelve years before she took to shore. Take her this chest and letter. It is not a grand adventure, but it will give you a passage to make, a harbour to find, and a friendly face at the end of it."`
                choice
                    `"I'll take it. And thank you for the lesson."`
                        accept
                    `"Not yet. I have a few things to learn in port first."`
                        close
    on accept
        log "Captain Elias Ward gave you his sea chest and a letter for Mara Bell in Tortuga. His advice covers sailing, rowing, docking, work, treasure, combat, fleets, and sea traffic."
    on visit
        conversation
            `Mara Bell is waiting beneath the awning of a chandlery in Tortuga. She recognises the brass clasp on the sea chest before you have said a word.`
            `"Elias has finally done it, then," she says. "Retired at last. He always said the sea would have to throw him off before he left it." She takes the letter carefully. "You have done him a kindness. And he has done you one, by the sound of it. Keep his advice. It is worth more than the chest."`
    on complete
        conversation
            `Mara presses a small purse into your hand. "For the passage, captain. And a little extra for listening to an old sailor. If you stay in these waters, you will find plenty of honest work, and enough trouble to keep you sharp."`
            `Outside, the harbour is busy with vessels coming and going. For the first time, the whole sea seems open to you.`
            `Mara catches your sleeve before you leave. "One more thing. If you put back into Port Royal, ask after Harbourmaster Briggs. He has been trying to find a cartographer named Isabel Crowe. I believe he has a fair sum to offer whoever can bring her home."`
        log "Mara Bell suggested returning to Port Royal to ask Harbourmaster Briggs about the missing cartographer Isabel Crowe."
